h the 1960s-1970s, reaching its lowest point in the 1980s. This suggests approximately 2-3 local maxima across the century-long period.\n\nThe next thinking continues the analysis of local maxima in the saving rate:\n\nThe Gross Saving as a Percent of GDP line from 1960-1988 reveals a nuanced economic trajectory. After the stable 1960s, the 1970s experienced an initial peak around 1975-1976, followed by a decline. A secondary recovery emerged in the late 1970s to early 1980s, with another peak around 1984-1985, ultimately returning to lower levels by the late 1980s. This pattern indicates 2 local maxima during this period.\n\nCombining both plots, the total local maxima reach 4-5 distinct points. However, I'll need to verify the specific characteristics of each plot in Exhibit 1, which includes two line graphs: \"Growth of Real GDP per Employee\" and \"Gross Saving as a Percent of GDP\".\n\nFor the \"Growth of Real GDP per Employee\" from 1960-1988, the trajectory shows significant variation. The graph rises from around 2% in the early 1960s, experiences a peak in the late 1960s to early 1970s at approximately 3%, then declines sharply during the 1974-1975 period, recovers in 1976-1978, and drops again in the early 1980s.\n\nThe \"Gross Saving as a Percent of GDP\" demonstrates a consistent downward trend. Starting at 10-11% in 1960, it gradually falls to 8-9% by the mid-1970s, further declines to 6-7% around 1980, experiences a slight recovery to 7-8% in the mid-1980s, and ultimately settles at 6-7% by the late 1980s.\n\nAnalyzing local maxima reveals one clear peak in the GDP growth plot and potential turning points in the saving rate graph, totaling approximately 2 local maxima.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_442656d0ad1442b2b04eb76d","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport json\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1990_09.json', 'r') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\nelements = data['document']['elements']\n# Let's see all figure elements and what pages they're on\nfigures = []\nfor i, e in enumerate(elements):\n    if e.get('type') == 'figure':\n        bbox = e.get('bbox')\n        if bbox:\n            page_id = bbox[0].get('page_id')\n            figures.append((i, page_id))\nprint('Figures on each page:')\nfor i, p in figures:\n    print(f'  Element {i}: page {p}')\n\""}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
